=== axis 11 (ops) — secret masking in the journal, and "what got blocked recently" ===

--- Probe 1: does a denied PreToolUse call ever get journaled? ---
$ grep -n "appendEvent" core/src/hook.ts
(no output — zero matches)
Confirmed: hook.ts's `deny()` (core/src/hook.ts:645-657) and every call site of it only
construct and return a JSON object for THIS tool call's stdout; nothing in hook.ts ever calls
appendEvent. A PreToolUse denial is not persisted anywhere. The only trace of it existing is
whatever the live chat surfaces at the moment it happens.

--- Probe 2: is there a CLI command to view recent denials / a block history? ---
$ node bin/harness --help
  ... report <sub>    Rendered views over the ledger, registry and gates. ...
  (no `events`, `history`, `audit`, `log`, or `blocked` command anywhere in the command list)
$ node bin/harness report --help
  Subcommands: packet <P> | rtm | rendere hub
  (packet/rtm/hub — none of these show hook decisions/denials)
Conclusion: there is no command anywhere that answers "what did the harness block recently?" —
only state-CHANGING actions (gate-submitted, wave-created, doctor-repaired, etc.) are journaled
and therefore reportable; enforcement actions (the actual point of the product) are not.

--- Probe 3: do free-text CLI flags that DO get journaled carry secrets verbatim? ---
$ node bin/harness loop critical raise --reason external-blocker \
    --detail "blocked by API key sk-FAKE-SECRET-abc123XYZ leaking in logs, need rotate"
"Escalation raised — exit code 2 means \"a human was summoned\", not failure. ..."
exit=2

$ cat .harness/events.jsonl
{"ts":"2026-08-27T14:44:43.380not json garbage {{{
{"ts":"2026-08-27T15:03:46.207Z","type":"critical-raised","data":{"reason":"external-blocker","detail":"blocked by API key sk-FAKE-SECRET-abc123XYZ leaking in logs, need rotate"}}

The fake secret string landed byte-for-byte in the append-only journal. No masking/redaction
of secret-shaped substrings (API keys, bearer tokens, etc.) exists anywhere in
core/src/events.ts's appendEvent path (read in full — it only JSON-stringifies + appends).
Source: core/src/loop.ts:286-293 (`const detail = (opts.detail ?? '').trim() ...; const data =
{ reason: opts.reason, detail }; ... appendEvent(root, 'critical-raised', data);`).

For contrast, the MOST COMMON journaled action (turn logs) does NOT carry free text into the
journal — `wave-turn-logged` only stores `{ id }` (core/src/wave.ts:284); the actual turn-log
prose lives in the wave's .md file, not the append-only journal. So this is not a systemic
"the whole journal is full of prose" problem — it is specific to the handful of event types
that accept a `--detail`/`--rationale`/`--reason` free-text flag and store it directly
(confirmed for `critical-raised`; `adr-proposed`/`adr-decided` in core/src/adr.ts pass
`rationale`/rejection reasons through a similar unguarded path).

--- Probe 4: is .harness/ (and therefore events.jsonl) gitignored by default? ---
$ find <sandbox-proj> -maxdepth 1 -name ".gitignore"
(no output — no top-level .gitignore was created by `harness init`)
$ ls .harness/.runtime/
.gitignore   hook-errors.log
Only `.harness/.runtime/.gitignore` exists (confirmed via core/src/cli.ts:968's reference to
that specific file — the only gitignore-related code path in the whole cli.ts). It scopes only
the `.runtime/` subdirectory (hook-errors.log, activity markers). `.harness/events.jsonl`,
`state.json`, `config.yaml`, `design/`, `waves/` are NOT gitignored — they are trackable/
committable by default. README.md:37 frames the journal as "Durable event journal; survives
`/clear`, resume, machine change" with no described sync mechanism other than the project's own
git history, so committing `.harness/` is the implied way this "survives machine change".

Combined with Probe 3: a secret pasted into `--detail` (or `--rationale`/`--reason` on the
ADR/backtrack/similar commands) becomes a permanent plaintext record that, by default, flows
straight into `git add .` / `git commit` the same way any other tracked file would — the exact
failure class the project's own README (line 253) says it gitleaks-scans ITS OWN 307 commits
for (0 found), but for which no equivalent safeguard exists for the journal THIS tool writes
into a user's project.
